{{also|Potash}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|nl|potasch}} (modern spelling {{m|nl|potas}}), coined in 1598. The literal translation is ''[[pot]] [[ash]]'', because it was made by burning wood to ashes in a large pot. First attested in 1648.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|/pɒt.æʃ/|lang=en}} {{attention|en|needs stress mark}}

===Noun===
{{wikipedia}}
{{en-noun|~|es}}

# the [[water]]-[[soluble]] part of the [[ash]] formed by [[burning]] [[plant]] [[material]]; used for making [[soap]], [[glass]] and as a [[fertilizer]]
# {{lb|en|chemistry}} an [[impure]] form of [[potassium]] [[carbonate]] (K<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub>) mixed with other potassium [[salt]]s
# {{lb|en|chemistry|archaic}} in the names of compounds of the form "... of potash", potassium (for example, "permanganate of potash" = [[potassium permanganate]])

